@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference){#bench-art .hl{transition:x .25s,width .25s}#bench-art .knob{transition:cx .25s}}</style></head><body><main class="lab"><header><p class="kicker">Computing model (closest science: sampling), Form 4 and 5</p><h1>Chunk size and finding the right passage</h1><p class="try"><strong>Try this:</strong> Drag the round handle under the bar left and right to change the chunk size.</p></header><div class="top"><section class="card idea"><h2>Key idea</h2><p>This is computing, not science, so this lab uses the closest science idea: how the size of a sampling window decides what it catches. A document is cut into equal chunks, and the chunk that best overlaps the answer is the one retrieval would find. Small chunks split the answer. Big chunks bury it in other text.</p><p>coverage = words of answer in best chunk / 60 x 100, purity = words of answer in best chunk / chunk size x 100</p></section><section class="card predict"><h2>Predict first</h2><p>The answer is 60 words long inside a 600 word document. What happens to the best chunk as chunks get much bigger?</p><ul><li>It always holds more of the answer and is always a better match</li><li>It holds all of the answer, but less of the chunk is the answer</li><li>It holds less of the answer</li></ul><details><summary>Check your prediction</summary><p>Drag the chunk size from 20 up to 300 and watch both percentages.</p></details></section></div><section class="card steps"><h2>Steps</h2><ol><li>Drag the handle to the far left (20 words) and watch how much of the answer the best chunk holds.</li><li>Drag it to about 80 words and watch the coverage reach 100 percent while the purity is still fairly high.</li><li>Drag it to the far right (300 words) and watch the purity fall as the answer is lost in other text.</li></ol></section><section class="benchrow" aria-label="The lab bench"><div class="bench"><svg id="bench-art" viewBox="0 0 400 210" role="img" aria-label="A 600 word document cut into chunks">
<text x="20" y="28">Document: 600 words, cut into chunks</text>
<rect class="doc" x="20" y="60" width="360" height="40"/>
<rect class="hl" id="art-hl" x="20" y="52" width="48" height="56"/>
<rect class="ans" x="170" y="66" width="36" height="28"/>
<path class="tick" id="art-ticks" d=""/>
<text x="20" y="128">Orange block: the 60 word answer. Shaded box: best chunk.</text>
<line class="track" x1="40" y1="170" x2="360" y2="170"/>
<circle class="knob" id="art-knob" data-synapse-handle="chunk" aria-hidden="true" style="touch-action:none;cursor:grab" cx="64" cy="170" r="24"/>
<text x="20" y="206">Chunk size (words)</text>
</svg></div><div class="side"><div class="readouts"><div class="readout"><div class="name" id="cover-name">Answer inside best chunk (%)</div><output id="cover" data-synapse-readout="cover" aria-labelledby="cover-name"></output></div><div class="readout"><div class="name" id="pure-name">Share of best chunk that is answer (%)</div><output id="pure" data-synapse-readout="pure" aria-labelledby="pure-name"></output></div><div class="readout"><div class="name" id="count-name">Number of chunks</div><output id="count" data-synapse-readout="count" aria-labelledby="count-name"></output></div></div><p id="found" class="card found" aria-live="polite"></p><details class="card fine"><summary>Keyboard controls</summary><div class="row"><label for="chunk">Chunk size</label><input type="range" id="chunk" data-synapse-control="chunk" min="20" max="300" step="10" value="40" data-unit="words" aria-valuetext="40 words"></div></details></div></section><section class="card hints"><h2>Need a hint?</h2><details><summary>Hint 1</summary><p>The shaded box is the best chunk. Compare its width with the small orange answer block.</p></details><details><summary>Hint 2</summary><p>Try 60, 70 and 80 words. Where the cuts fall matters, so the best size is not always the same as the answer length.</p></details></section><section class="explain"><h2>Why it happens</h2><p>Chunks are cut at fixed places. A chunk that is too small can cut the answer in two, so no single chunk holds all of it. This is why coverage is low for small chunks.</p><p>A chunk that is too large holds the whole answer, but most of its words are about other things. Its match to the question is weaker, so purity falls. A middle size, a little longer than the answer, usually balances the two. Where the cuts fall also matters, so the results do not change smoothly.</p></section></main><script>
addEventListener("message", function (e) {
